1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,584
Right now,

2
00:00:00,584 --> 00:00:03,098
a new college graduate is more likely to be

3
00:00:03,098 --> 00:00:05,728
out of work than the average American worker.

4
00:00:05,728 --> 00:00:08,533
Five point two percent, against four point zero.

5
00:00:08,653 --> 00:00:12,794
And the jobs that used to start a career are the ones AI is getting good at.

6
00:00:12,794 --> 00:00:14,755
So is college still worth the money?

7
00:00:15,105 --> 00:00:18,439
That first number is real, but it answers the wrong question.

8
00:00:18,439 --> 00:00:20,952
A family deciding about college isn't choosing

9
00:00:20,952 --> 00:00:23,084
between a degree and an average worker.

10
00:00:23,084 --> 00:00:25,707
They're choosing between a degree and no degree.

11
00:00:25,827 --> 00:00:27,365
So put those side by side.

12
00:00:27,365 --> 00:00:28,134
In September,

13
00:00:28,134 --> 00:00:32,867
young workers without a degree had a seven point four percent unemployment rate.

14
00:00:32,867 --> 00:00:35,470
Recent college grads were at five point two.

15
00:00:35,590 --> 00:00:38,395
And the pay gap is wider than the job gap.

16
00:00:38,395 --> 00:00:44,006
In 2025, grads aged 22 to 27 earned a median of about sixty thousand dollars a year.

17
00:00:44,006 --> 00:00:47,479
Young high school grads earned about forty thousand.

18
00:00:47,479 --> 00:00:49,883
That's one and a half times as much.

19
00:00:50,003 --> 00:00:53,246
On the two numbers that pay the bills, the degree still wins.

20
00:00:53,596 --> 00:00:57,731
The worry about AI is real, but it's narrower than the headlines.

21
00:00:57,731 --> 00:01:02,057
A Stanford team looked at payroll records for workers aged 22 to 25.

22
00:01:02,057 --> 00:01:04,029
In the jobs most exposed to AI,

23
00:01:04,029 --> 00:01:08,864
employment for that age group was about nineteen percent lower by June 2026,

24
00:01:08,864 --> 00:01:11,981
compared with young workers in less exposed jobs.

25
00:01:11,981 --> 00:01:14,780
A year earlier, the gap was fifteen percent.

26
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:19,141
The same study finds the drop comes from fewer hires, not more layoffs.

27
00:01:19,141 --> 00:01:23,143
And it finds no evidence of widespread job loss across the economy.

28
00:01:23,143 --> 00:01:26,249
The authors call these patterns, not proof of cause.

29
00:01:26,369 --> 00:01:28,865
So the part that's shrinking is the first rung.

30
00:01:28,865 --> 00:01:31,786
That matters for a new grad, but it's a hiring problem,

31
00:01:31,786 --> 00:01:33,857
not a sign that degrees stopped paying.

32
00:01:34,207 --> 00:01:37,640
Here's the number that should worry parents more than AI.

33
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:41,917
Among all recent grads, thirty-nine percent were underemployed in 2024,

34
00:01:41,917 --> 00:01:45,109
meaning they worked a job that doesn't need a degree.

35
00:01:45,229 --> 00:01:48,201
And the average hides a huge spread by major.

36
00:01:48,201 --> 00:01:53,219
In 2024, special education grads had an unemployment rate under one percent.

37
00:01:53,219 --> 00:01:55,134
Nursing was at two point one.

38
00:01:55,134 --> 00:01:56,785
Finance, two point eight.

39
00:01:56,785 --> 00:01:58,370
Computer science, seven.

40
00:01:58,490 --> 00:02:01,007
Underemployment spreads out the same way.

41
00:02:01,007 --> 00:02:03,523
Computer science grads who land a job are

42
00:02:03,523 --> 00:02:06,162
underemployed nineteen percent of the time.

43
00:02:06,162 --> 00:02:08,004
Art history grads, forty-five.

44
00:02:08,004 --> 00:02:09,477
Philosophy, forty-seven.

45
00:02:09,597 --> 00:02:11,142
So "college" isn't one bet.

46
00:02:11,142 --> 00:02:14,290
It's dozens of different bets with very different odds.

47
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:18,480
You could read all this and decide the safe move is to skip college.

48
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:20,174
The data doesn't support that.

49
00:02:20,174 --> 00:02:21,247
In the same tables,

50
00:02:21,247 --> 00:02:25,200
the no-degree path has the higher unemployment rate and the lower pay.

51
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:27,637
What AI changes is the margin for error.

52
00:02:27,637 --> 00:02:29,548
When entry-level hiring tightens,

53
00:02:29,548 --> 00:02:33,661
the major and the plan for the first job matter more than they used to.

54
00:02:34,011 --> 00:02:36,266
So, is college still worth it with AI?

55
00:02:36,266 --> 00:02:37,216
On average, yes.

56
00:02:37,216 --> 00:02:41,905
A degree still means lower unemployment and about one and a half times the pay.

57
00:02:42,025 --> 00:02:44,176
But the average is doing a lot of work.

58
00:02:44,176 --> 00:02:47,705
The first rung is getting harder in jobs where AI does the task,

59
00:02:47,705 --> 00:02:50,131
and the payoff depends heavily on the major.

60
00:02:50,251 --> 00:02:54,251
So the question for your family isn't "college or not?" It's "which degree,

61
00:02:54,251 --> 00:02:56,331
and what's the plan for the first job?"
